---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import IpAddressLookup from '@/components/tools/reference/IpAddressLookup.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="ip-address-lookup">
  <IpAddressLookup client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What the IP address lookup shows</h2>
    <p>
      The IP address lookup finds the network that announces an address on the internet: its
      autonomous system number (ASN), the AS name and organisation, the country where the network is
      registered, and the routed range that contains the address. It works for IPv4 and IPv6.
    </p>
    <p>
      The country is the registration country of the network, not the place of a device. A cloud,
      mobile or VPN network can use one range in many countries. Private and special addresses, such
      as 192.168.x.x, 10.x.x.x and fe80::, are never routed on the internet, so they have no network.
    </p>
    <h2>How the lookup runs in your browser</h2>
    <p>
      The table of routed ranges is split into small files on this site, each one with a few thousand
      ranges in order. The page finds the right file with a binary search over the first address of
      each file, loads only that file, and searches it the same way. The address you type does not
      leave your browser.
    </p>
    <h2>The address in binary</h2>
    <p>
      An IPv4 address is 32 bits and an IPv6 address is 128 bits. The black bits are the network part
      of the routed block that contains the address; the grey bits are the host part. The CIDR suffix,
      for example <code>/24</code>, is the number of network bits.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
